# RENDERED by ansible/roles/k3s_config — do not edit this file on the host; edit
# the node's committed profile under
# ci-runner/k3s/phase0-bare-metal/profiles/ and re-apply ansible/ci-pool.yml.
#
# This node's own kubelet arguments, rendered from the records its profile
# declares in KUBELET_ARGS. k3s merges every file in
# /etc/rancher/k3s/config.yaml.d/ over /etc/rancher/k3s/config.yaml and reads the
# result on EVERY start, so these survive a reboot and a re-registration.
#
# `kubelet-arg+` AND NOT `kubelet-arg`, AND THE `+` IS LOAD-BEARING. The shared
# ci-runner/k3s/phase2/k3s-config/config.agent.yaml that EVERY agent receives
# already declares `kubelet-arg` carrying `max-pods=200`, a per-node pod bound
# with its own derivation (phase2/kueue/DERIVATION.md "The pod-capacity
# constraint"). k3s's multi-file merge REPLACES a list-valued key a later file
# re-declares and APPENDS only when that key carries a `+` suffix — so the plain
# key here would silently drop `max-pods` on exactly the node whose kubelet is
# most constrained. The repository test
# tests/livespec_dev_tooling/test_hp_coexistence_budget.py fails if this ever
# loses the suffix.
#
# RENDERED ONLY FOR A NODE THAT DECLARES SOME. Unlike the node-taint drop-in
# beside this one — where an empty list positively states "this node is open",
# which is the fact a re-registration has to read — an empty `kubelet-arg+` says
# nothing at all, so the role's task is gated on a non-empty KUBELET_ARGS rather
# than rendering an empty list.
kubelet-arg+: {{ k3s_config_kubelet_arg_records }}
